artifact: s37_shipping_step0.c62c024714f2.{pof,svf}
purpose: STEP 0 of the staged bring-up (S36 §3). S38 flashes this, once PW
  has ruled on the AN_EN interlock (S35-1). Nothing else is in it.

SHIPPING: yes — no non-shipping switch is set.

built_from: 61e38ce34f27d0b1052c57a401dd973355c724d5
  branch s37-step0-shipping-base = a4ee3d1f + the converter clock pair
  (7ac01126, S35) + the X-logic parking (61e38ce3, S36's fix on this base).
  THIS BRANCH MUST NEVER BE MERGED TO main. It is the shipping design that
  is on the part today plus two zero-cost fixes, deliberately WITHOUT main's
  247 later logic elements — see "why not main" below.

config: (none) — a4ee3d1f's build.sh predates CFG_LINE entirely, which is the
  earliest of the four cfg-line shapes (S36 §0). Every non-shipping switch is
  off, so this is the shipping configuration; the absent cfg line is a
  property of that build.sh, not a switch position.

device: 5M1270ZT144C4
logic_elements: 156 / 1,270 (12 %)
pins: 68 / 114 (60 %)
fmax: 66.1 MHz
sim_gate: PASS (5 testbenches, both new gates proved to bite by mutation)
slot_map: sha256:efd8d555440094b627345b5a84894f9e7a6b569056f4eccb417d42d6ce32182e

md5_pof: 79284dad6e9c27995f91806056c9fba5
md5_svf: d47b74c6c881b82a110491266a6bd31d

reproducibility: built TWICE from a clean `git archive` of the branch into a
  fresh scratch tree. The two pof files are BYTE-IDENTICAL. The two svf files
  differ in exactly one line — `!Device #1: ... <wall-clock>`, an SVF comment
  (`!`) emitted by quartus_cpf. With comment lines removed the svf bodies are
  identical, so the bytes openocd plays are reproducible; the pof is the
  artifact to hash. This applies to every artifact in this directory and is
  why S37 asserts byte-identity on the pof.

design_id: NONE — AND THAT IS NOT FIXABLE AT THIS SIZE.
  S37's dispatch asked for a readable design ID on this artifact. It cannot
  have one. The design-ID/knock readback block arrived at `3152e2b1` and costs
  +124 LEs (S36 §1.1); adding it here would take the step-0 bitstream from 156
  to ~280 LEs and put never-benched RTL into the one flash the whole staged
  plan exists to keep clean. So this artifact identifies itself exactly as the
  bitstream it replaces does, and as its own rollback does:

    a1f6672af6c3  (the shipping bitstream, on the part today)  no design ID
    s37 step 0    (this)                                        no design ID

  The identification for S38 is therefore the md5 above, recorded by
  `logic_flash.sh` on the desk AND on the bench before the first byte is
  written, plus the flash log. The design ID becomes readable at step 2 (S39),
  which is the first step that carries main's RTL.

why_not_main: main after S37's merges is 403 LEs in the shipping
  configuration (`dsp4_logic.4f702a181b61`, also staged here). Flashing that
  as step 0 would put 247 logic elements that have never run in a shipping
  configuration onto the part in the SAME operation as a two-pin change, and
  if anything moved, nothing in the method could say which change moved it.
  That is exactly the confound S35-3 found and S36 §3 was written to avoid.
  `4f702a181b61` is step 2 / S39's artifact, not step 0's.

rollback: dsp4_logic.a1f6672af6c3.svf
  md5 dd1e09185804cb2e451d5089cdd56be3 (svf) / f08f3b525ff0fe2f7957a96d958842e6 (pof)

S38_COMMAND_LINE:
  cd tools/pi && ./logic_flash.sh --dry-run s37_shipping_step0.c62c024714f2.svf
  # then, and only once PW has ruled on AN_EN, ONE of:
  #   ./logic_flash.sh --stop-app          s37_shipping_step0.c62c024714f2.svf
  #   ./logic_flash.sh --an-en-waived "PW <date>: <written reason>" \
  #                                        s37_shipping_step0.c62c024714f2.svf
  # the rollback defaults to dsp4_logic.a1f6672af6c3.svf, which is correct.

S38_PROOF (S36 §3, step 0):
  * the probe: J18 P37 = 12.288 MHz, P38 = 48 kHz — dead today. Or U3 pins
    142/141 at the source. This is the measurement that closes S34-1.
  * blk_* 30 s, zero overruns, both chips.
  * NOT the latency bar: this base has no capture path (pcm_din is tied to
    1'b0), so the arm cannot run at all. That is step 1's.
